  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/lonchogenys_ilisha_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lonchogenys ilisha is an extremely unusual tetra and a member of the family Acestrorhynchidae, which means the species is more closely related to the barracuda tetras (Acestrorhynchus) than, for example, to a cardinal tetra. The maximum length reported is 6-7 cm; the imported specimens were 5-7 cm long and most probably almost fully grown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/lonchogenys_ilisha_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lonchogenys ilisha is the only known member of its genus. It occurs in the upper and middle Rio Negro region in Brazil and Colombia. The imported specimens originate from Colombia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/lonchogenys_ilisha_en/",
      "html": "<p>First observations showed the species to be very peaceful, both towards conspecifics and towards other fish of similar size.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/lonchogenys_ilisha_en/",
      "html": "<p>Specimens with a golden shine and a bright orange caudal fin are thought to be the males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/lonchogenys_ilisha_en/",
      "html": "<p>No information is available so far regarding the aquarium biology of this species.</p>"
    }
  ],
